      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** instruments | **Difficulty:** 2/10 | **Time:** ~12 min"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "The guitar is a fretted chordophone — six strings standard (E-A-D-G-B-E low to high), though seven, eight, and twelve-string variants exist. Origins trace to medieval lute family; modern classical guitar crystallised in 19th-century Spain."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "types",
      "title": "Types",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Classical (nylon strings, fingerstyle). Steel-string acoustic (folk, country). Electric (magnetic pickups, amplification). Resonator (Dobro) for slide. Flamenco guitar — lighter, tap plate."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "technique",
      "title": "Technique",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Right hand: fingerpicking (p-i-m-a) or plectrum. Left hand: frets fix pitch; barre chords across strings. Capo transposes open shapes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "in-trad",
      "title": "In trad",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Backing chords in sessions; DADGAD and open tunings for Celtic accompaniment. Flatpicking for reels; fingerstyle for airs."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
